{UTF-8}
{ Chapter 13: a population that ages. Fourteen age classes of five years; every five years each class moves up to the next.
  How many people are 65 or older in 2040, and how fast does the share of old people grow? }
age : a0, a5, a10, a15, a20, a25, a30, a35, a40, a45, a50, a55, a60, a65plus ~ ~ |
Start population[age]=
	60, 62, 64, 66, 68, 70, 68, 64, 60, 56, 50, 44, 36, 60
	~	thousand people
	~	People in each class in 2020.
	|
Mortality[age]=
	0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.003, 0.003, 0.003, 0.003, 0.01, 0.05
	~	1/year
	~	The share of each class that dies each year.
	|
Fertility=
	0.045
	~	1/year
	~	Births a year per person aged 20 to 39.
	|
Births per year=
	Fertility * (Population[a20] + Population[a25] + Population[a30] + Population[a35])
	~	thousand people/year
	~	New people. They join the youngest class when the classes move up.
	|
Population[age]= INTEG(
	Change[age],
	Start population[age])
	~	thousand people
	~	~	|
Change[age]=
	AGE(Population, every=5, entrants=Births per year) - Mortality[age] * Population[age]
	~	thousand people/year
	~	The classes move up every five years and the youngest is refilled with the births of the period; deaths in every class.
	|
Total=
	SUM(Population[age!])
	~	thousand people
	~	Everyone.
	|
Old share=
	Population[a65plus] / Total
	~	dmnl
	~	The share of people who are 65 or older.
	|
INITIAL TIME = 0 ~ year ~ |
FINAL TIME = 50 ~ year ~ |
TIME STEP = 1 ~ year ~ |
SAVEPER = 1 ~ year ~ |
